[LINK: (e) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Special:EditPage/Template:Party_politics]

A one-party state, single-party state, one-party system or single-party system is a governance structure in which only a single political party controls the ruling system. [LINK: ([1]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/One-party_state#cite_note-1]  In a one-party state, all opposition parties are either outlawed or have limited and controlled participation in  [LINK: (elections) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Election] . The expression "de facto one-party" is sometimes used to describe a dominant-party system that, unlike a one-party state, allows (at least nominally) multiparty elections, but the existing practices or balance of  [LINK: (political power) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Political_power]  effectively prevent the  [LINK: (opposition) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Opposition_(politics)]  from winning power. [LINK: ([2]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/One-party_state#cite_note-2]

Membership in the ruling party tends to be relatively small compared to the population. [LINK: ([3]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/One-party_state#cite_note-3]  Rather, they give out private goods to fellow  [LINK: (elites) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Elite]  to ensure continued support. One-party, compared to dominant-party  [LINK: (dictatorships) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dictatorship] , structure themselves unlike democracies. They also turn into multi-party democracies at a lower rate than dominant-party dictatorships. [LINK: ([4]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/One-party_state#cite_note-Political_Order_and_One-Party_Rule-4]  While one-party states prohibit opposition parties, some allow for independent candidates to stand for election in competition with party candidates. Therefore, they place elites and sympathetic candidates in key administrative races. [LINK: ([5]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/One-party_state#cite_note-dx.doi.org-5]  For example, the  [LINK: (Chinese Communist Party) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chinese_Communist_Party]  exercises political control by infiltrating village administrations. [LINK: ([6]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/One-party_state#cite_note-6]  They view these positions as crucial for gathering  [LINK: (information on the population) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Information_on_the_population&action=edit&redlink=1]  and maintaining a presence in the far reaches of their borders. [LINK: ([7]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/One-party_state#cite_note-The_Single-Party_Dictator's_Dilemma_2011-7]

One-party states recognize the trade-off between election victory and  [LINK: (gathering) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Data_collection]  valuable data. [LINK: ([8]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/One-party_state#cite_note-8]  To account for this, the governments have been observed placing local nobility in easy-to-win races. [LINK: ([9]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/One-party_state#cite_note-9]  One-party states have also been observed using elections to ensure that only the most popular elites get chosen to office. [LINK: ([10]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/One-party_state#cite_note-Elections_Under_Authoritarianism-10]  They also gather data from elections to indicate if a local official is performing poorly in the eyes of the residents. [LINK: ([10]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/One-party_state#cite_note-Elections_Under_Authoritarianism-10]  This gives locals the opportunity to monitor local officials and communicate satisfaction with the local government. [LINK: ([10]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/One-party_state#cite_note-Elections_Under_Authoritarianism-10]  Throughout the country, members of the one party hold key political positions. [LINK: ([4]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/One-party_state#cite_note-Political_Order_and_One-Party_Rule-4]  In doing so, the party avoids committing outright fraud and rather sustains their power at the local level with strategic appointment of elites. [LINK: ([7]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/One-party_state#cite_note-The_Single-Party_Dictator's_Dilemma_2011-7]  Data on one-party governments can be difficult to gather given their lack of transparency. [LINK: ([5]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/One-party_state#cite_note-dx.doi.org-5]


