# NOTES #

[LINK: (^) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/One-party_state#cite_ref-23]  Previously known as the  [LINK: (Democratic Republic of Vietnam) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Democratic_Republic_of_Vietnam]  from 2 September 1945 to 25 April 1976. [LINK: ([20]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/One-party_state#cite_note-20]   [LINK: (Reunified with) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Reunification_Day]  the  [LINK: (Republic of South Vietnam) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Provisional_Revolutionary_Government_of_the_Republic_of_South_Vietnam]  on 2 July 1976. [LINK: ([21]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/One-party_state#cite_note-21]  [LINK: ([22]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/One-party_state#cite_note-22]

[LINK: (^) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/One-party_state#cite_ref-Zhongguo_51-0]  The  [LINK: (Republic of China) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Republic_of_China_(1912%E2%80%931949)]  controlled the  [LINK: (mainland) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mainland_China]  from 1912 to 1949 and the island of Taiwan since 1945, in which the ROC currently administers  [LINK: (the Free area) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Free_area_of_the_Republic_of_China] . This government served China in the  [LINK: (United Nations) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_Nations]  from 1945 to 1971. See  [LINK: (Political status of Taiwan) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Political_status_of_Taiwan]  and the  [LINK: (One-China policy) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/One_China]  for various viewpoints.

[LINK: (^) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/One-party_state#cite_ref-60]  Following the appointment of  [LINK: (Marcelo Caetano) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Marcelo_Caetano]  as Prime Minister in 1968, the opposition was allowed to run to the  [LINK: (1969 Portuguese legislative election) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/1969_Portuguese_legislative_election] , before being banned.

[LINK: (^) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/One-party_state#cite_ref-66]  While the 2012 constitution introduced by  [LINK: (Bashar al-Assad) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bashar_al-Assad]  theoretically enabled the establishment of political parties, Ba'athist Syria remained a de-facto one-party state with an extensive  [LINK: (secret police) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/General_Intelligence_Directorate_(Syria)]  apparatus that curtails independent political activities.


